K is a general prism with axis . ... ### The logarithmic Minkowski problem for polytopes\n\n## Advances in Mathematics\n\nDate: May 2026\n\nArticle: 110886\n\nVolume: Volume 492\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n# Sharp quantitative stability for the Minkowski first inequality via a quadratic estimate for the cone-volume measure☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Yu-De Liu, Ge Xiong, Kai-Wen Yang\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.aim.2026.110886\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nA nearly optimal quadratic estimate for the cone-volume measure at antipodal points is obtained. As applications of this estimate, sharp quantitative stability results are established for the Minkowski first inequality, the Brunn-Minkowski inequality, and the reverse isoperimetric inequality associated with the Lutwak-Yang-Zhang functional.\n\n## MSC\n\n52A40\n\n;\n\n26D15\n\n## Keywords\n\nConvex body\n\n;\n\nCone-volume measure\n\n;\n\nBrunn-Minkowski inequality\n\n;\n\nMinkowski first inequality\n\n## Introduction\n\nThe setting for this paper is the n-dimensional Euclidean space, {\\mathbb{R}}^{n}. A convex body is a compact convex set that has a nonempty interior. More intriguingly, we reveal that this optimal estimate can be used to establish sharp quantitative stability for the Minkowski first inequality, for the Brunn-Minkowski inequality and for the reverse isoperimetric inequality of the Lutwak-Yang-Zhang functional.\n\nTo state our main results, we list some notions.\n\nLet K and L be convex bodies in {\\mathbb{R}}^{n}. Write , and is called the symmetric difference for mixed volumes of K and L.\n\nTheorem 1.1\n\nIf K is a convex body in {\\mathbb{R}}^{n} with its centroid at the origin and , then with equality if and only if K is a general prism with axis .\n\nTheorem 1.2\n\nIf K and L are convex bodies in {\\mathbb{R}}^{n}, then with equality if and only if K and L are homothetic.\n\nTo prove Theorem 1.1, we adopt more refined geometric deformation than above mentioned to estimate \\frac{{V}_{K}(\\{u\\})}{{V}_{n}(K)} and \\frac{{V}_{K}(\\{-u\\})}{{V}_{n}(K)} in Section 3. To be specific, from the deformation of the Schwartz symmetrization of the original polytope to a frustum of a cone in [37], the lower base of the Schwartz symmetrization, the width in the direction doing Schwartz symmetrization and the volume of the polytope are preserved, but the position of centroid is changed. In this article, we find a unique desired frustum of a cone, which preserves not only the width in the direction doing Schwartz symmetrization and volume of the original convex body, but also the position of centroid. See Lemma 3.2 for details. It is worth mentioning that the position of centroid of the desired frustum of a cone is the same as the original convex body is so critical that we can estimate \\frac{{V}_{K}(\\{u\\})}{{V}_{n}(K)} and \\frac{{V}_{K}(\\{-u\\})}{{V}_{n}(K)} via the frustum of a cone. Consequently, we prove Theorem 3.5, which contains accurate estimates for \\frac{{V}_{K}(\\{u\\})}{{V}_{n}(K)} and \\frac{{V}_{K}(\\{-u\\})}{{V}_{n}(K)}. Moreover, Theorem 3.6 demonstrates these estimates are indeed optimal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLogarithmic Brunn--Minkowski Inequality under $n-2$ Reflection Symmetries (https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.20730)\nciteturn7academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; We prove the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality for origin-symmetric convex bodies with symmetries to n-2 orthogonal hyperplanes, and discuss the equality case and the uniqueness of the related logarithmic Minkowski problem.\nTitle: Logarithmic Brunn--Minkowski Inequality under $n-2$ Reflection Symmetries\nAuthors: XiaoRui Lu\nDate: Fri Aug 21 04:22:15 2026\n\nWe prove the log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality for origin-symmetric convex bodies with symmetries to n-2 orthogonal hyperplanes, and discuss the equality case and the uniqueness of the related logarithmic Minkowski problem. We also generalize the inequality from the Lebesgue measure to certain log-concave measure.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuantitative stability of the Brunn-Minkowski inequality for sets of equal volume (https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/33e4b995-8239-4e97-bebf-29c08762994e)\nciteturn7search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; © 2017, Fudan University and Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg.The authors prove a quantitative stability result for the Brunn-Minkowski inequality on sets of equal volume: If |A| = |B| > 0 and |A + B|1/n = (2+δ)|A|1/n for some small δ, then, up to a translation, both A and B are close (in terms of δ) to a convex set K. ... Also, the result here provides a stronger estimate for the stability exponent than the previous result of the authors.\n\n# Quantitative stability of the Brunn-Minkowski inequality for sets of equal volume\n\nDownload\n\nName\n\nstability.BrM.pdf\n\nDescription\n\nAccepted version\n\nAuthor(s)\n\nFigalli, Alessio\n\n•\n\nJerison, David\n\nDate Issued\n\n2017\n\nJournal\n\nChinese Annals of Mathematics. Series B\n\nPublisher\n\nSpringer Nature\n\nCitation\n\nFigalli, Alessio, and David Jerison. \"Quantitative Stability of the Brunn-Minkowski Inequality for Sets of Equal Volume.\" Chinese Annals of Mathematics Series B 38 2 (2017): 393-412.\n\nVersion\n\nAuthor's final manuscript\n\nAbstract\n\n© 2017, Fudan University and Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg. The authors prove a quantitative stability result for the Brunn-Minkowski inequality on sets of equal volume: If |A| = |B| > 0 and |A + B|1/n = (2+δ)|A|1/n for some small δ, then, up to a translation, both A and B are close (in terms of δ) to a convex set K. Although this result was already proved by the authors in a previous paper, the present paper provides a more elementary proof that the authors believe has its own interest. Also, the result here provides a stronger estimate for the stability exponent than the previous result of the authors.\n\nMIT Department\n\nMassachusetts Institute of Technology. Department of Mathematics\n\nTerms of Use\n\nCreative Commons Attribution-Noncommercial-Share Alike\n\nhttp://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\n\nPersistent DSpace Link\n\nhttps://hdl.handle.net/1721.1/135700\n\nDOI of Published Version\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/S11401-017-1075-8--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChapter 4\n\nStability of log-Brunn-Minkowski and lo (https://www.etd.ceu.edu/2023/de_apratim.pdf)\nciteturn7search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.0 years ago; Stability of log-Brunn-Minkowski and log-Minkowski inequality under \\(n\\) hyperplane symmetries ... Conjecture 4.1.1 (Logarithmic Brunn-Minkowski conjecture). ... with equality if and only if \\(K = K_1 + \\cdots + K_m\\) and \\(C = C_1 + \\cdots + C_m\\) compact convex sets \\(K_1,\\ldots,K_m,C_1,\\ldots,C_m\\) of dimension at least one where \\(\\sum_{i=1}^m \\dim K_i = n\\) and \\(K_i\\) and \\(C_i\\) are dilates, \\(i=1,\\ldots,m\\).\nChapter 4\n\nStability of log-Brunn-Minkowski and log-Minkowski inequality under \\(n\\) hyperplane symmetries\n\n## 4.1 Introduction\n\nIn this chapter, we establish stability versions of the log-Minkowski and log-Brunn-Minkowski conjectures under many hyperplane symmetries. First, recall that for \\(\\lambda \\in (0,1)\\), the \\(L_0\\) or logarithmic sum of two origin symmetric convex bodies \\(K\\) and \\(C\\) in \\(\\mathbb{R}^n\\) is defined by\n\n\\[\n(1-\\lambda)\\cdot K +_0 \\lambda \\cdot C = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : \\langle x,u\\rangle \\le h_K(u)^{1-\\lambda} h_C(u)^\\lambda \\ \\forall u \\in S^{n-1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt is linearly invariant in the sense that \\(A((1-\\lambda)\\cdot K +_0 \\lambda \\cdot C) = (1-\\lambda)\\cdot AK +_0 \\lambda \\cdot AC\\) for \\(A \\in GL(n)\\). We recall here the long-standing and highly investigated equivalent conjectures - the log-Brunn-Minkowski and log-Minkowski conjecture for centered convex-bodies.\n\nConjecture 4.1.1 (Logarithmic Brunn-Minkowski conjecture). If \\(\\lambda \\in (0,1)\\) and \\(K\\) and \\(C\\) are convex bodies in \\(\\mathbb{R}^n\\) whose centroid is the origin, then\n\n\\[\nV((1-\\lambda)\\cdot K +_0 \\lambda \\cdot C) \\ge V(K)^{1-\\lambda}V(C)^\\lambda, \\qquad (4.1)\n\\]\n\nwith equality if and only if \\(K = K_1 + \\cdots + K_m\\) and \\(C = C_1 + \\cdots + C_m\\) compact convex sets \\(K_1,\\ldots,K_m,C_1,\\ldots,C_m\\) of dimension at least one where \\(\\sum_{i=1}^m \\dim K_i = n\\) and \\(K_i\\) and \\(C_i\\) are dilates, \\(i=1,\\ldots,m\\).\n\n75--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLog-Brunn-Minkowski inequality under symmetry (https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.12239)\nciteturn7academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.6 years ago; We prove the log-Brunn-Minkowski conjecture for convex bodies with symmetries to $n$ independent hyperplanes, and discuss the equality case and the uniqueness of the solution of the related case of the logarithmic Minkowski problem.\nTitle: Log-Brunn-Minkowski inequality under symmetry\nAuthors: Károly J. Böröczky, Pavlos Kalantzopoulos\nDate: Thu Feb 27 16:38:29 2020\n\nWe prove the log-Brunn-Minkowski conjecture for convex bodies with symmetries to $n$ independent hyperplanes, and discuss the equality case and the uniqueness of the solution of the related case of the logarithmic Minkowski problem. We also clarify a small gap in the known argument classifying the equality case of the log-Brunn-Minkowski conjecture for unconditional convex bodies.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuantitative stability of the Brunn-Minkowski (https://cvgmt.sns.it/media/doc/paper/3139/stability.BrM.pdf)\nciteturn7search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; The result above provides a general quantitative stability for the Brunn-Minkowski inequality ... However 